#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199057} :activity/id #uuid "b9abbff1-085d-5330-8eee-8472c1138825" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580 :topic/id #uuid "aad2999b-dacb-527f-a4a8-1fce51919fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4257} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199531 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$P\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\ & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$U = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is $$\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{2,3,5,7}}={1,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $$T_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={1,4,6,8,9}\\cup {1,3,9} \\\\ & ={1,3,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06a295ff-82fc-5afe-8aa1-b24421f2c474" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7992 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49a55e7b-da8f-5b9b-aca0-ea5672a75f90" :step/math-academy-id 144906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198114 :question/id #uuid "662bda92-63ee-5d5c-a45f-7219b160e08d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12740"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "264cfe66-691e-550e-97ad-b71924332f21" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381664}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of an Implication"} :step/id #uuid "5bf2cacb-1ce2-5c76-9cc6-0e6f2a29dc49" :step/math-academy-id 144907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199533 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true). $$x + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3$$ This statement is true because $$\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).$$\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false). $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2$$ This statement is false because $$\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).$$ However, the following statement is true: $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e9f21f0-8dbb-5442-968e-b1fb4425fef7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7995 :tutorial/title "Equivalence of Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "62147a48-4a50-55e4-a548-b950bc9af5a9" :step/math-academy-id 144910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199609 :tutorial/content "For predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ the equivalence $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nIt follows that the truth set of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ can be found using\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's find the truth set of $P(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $1-5x < 11$\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq -1$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the corresponding inequality: $$\\begin{aligned}1-5x & <11 \\\\ -5x & <10 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}$$ So, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = (-2, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_P = (-\\infty,-2].$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $T_Q = [-1, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_Q = (-\\infty,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-2,\\infty )\\cap [-1,\\infty ) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty ) \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cap (-\\infty ,-1) \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty )\\cup (-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [-1,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "196ed409-81bb-5e86-bf1c-5e34aa4fb041" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9146 :tutorial/title "Truth Sets of Equivalences"} :step/id #uuid "2f2be205-1493-56f5-8512-a42f382c50fa" :step/math-academy-id 144911 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198266 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198112 :question/id #uuid "a2ae75f0-e1bd-5f07-b728-7d574b892786" :question/math-academy-id "e-12738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c5f2a86-209a-5bb3-a3f2-77d679adb0a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050888}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390190}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of an Equivalence"} :step/id #uuid "4e2b4e61-5f88-5898-bcf9-9d4fae86c41e" :step/math-academy-id 144912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199532 :tutorial/content "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}P\\Rightarrow Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P\\Rightarrow Q)∧(Q\\Rightarrow P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "599dc11e-cbd2-596c-bb72-4f73b83034ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7993 :tutorial/title "Logical Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences With Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "b35ba584-56c8-537f-b6d7-15de6671592a" :step/math-academy-id 144915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198267 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198113 :question/id #uuid "316efd8a-8618-58b9-a41c-7507942f15b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12739"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401755}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"} :step/id #uuid "bc5d356c-d8d2-517d-839c-18b823820728" :step/math-academy-id 144916}] :activity/title "Conditional Statements With Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198522}]}
